      "text": "To find \\(\\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\), we will use the periodicity of the sine function. The sine function is periodic with a period of \\(2\\pi\\), meaning that \\(\\sin(x) = \\sin(x + 2k\\pi)\\) for any integer \\(k\\). We start by simplifying \\(\\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\) to a value within one period of \\(2\\pi\\).\n\nFirst, we reduce \\(\\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\) modulo \\(2\\pi\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{11\\pi}{3} = 3\\pi + \\frac{4\\pi}{3} = \\frac{9\\pi}{3} + \\frac{4\\pi}{3} = 3\\pi + \\frac{4\\pi}{3}\n\\]\nSince \\(3\\pi = 3\\pi - 2\\pi = \\pi\\), we can simplify the expression further:\n\\[\n\\frac{11\\pi}{3} = \\pi + \\frac{4\\pi}{3}\n\\]\nNow, since \\(\\sin(x + 2\\pi) = \\sin(x)\\), we can further simplify \\(\\frac{4\\pi}{3}\\) by subtracting \\(2\\pi\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{4\\pi}{3} = 2\\pi - \\frac{2\\pi}{3}\n\\]\nThus,\n\\[\n\\sin \\left( \\frac{11\\pi}{3} \\right) = \\sin \\left( \\pi + \\frac{4\\pi}{3} \\right) = \\sin \\left( 2\\pi - \\frac{2\\pi}{3} \\right)\n\\]\nUsing the identity \\(\\sin(\\pi - x) = \\sin(x)\\), we get:\n\\[\n\\sin \\left( 2\\pi - \\frac{2\\pi}{3} \\right) = -\\sin \\left( \\frac{2\\pi}{3} \\right)\n\\]\nNext, we need to find \\(\\sin \\left( \\frac{2\\pi}{3} \\right)\\). The angle \\(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\) is in the second quadrant, and the sine of an angle in the second quadrant is positive. The reference angle for \\(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\) is \\(\\pi - \\frac{2\\pi}{3} = \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\). Therefore,\n\\[\n\\sin \\left( \\frac{2\\pi}{3} \\right) = \\sin \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\right) = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\nThus,\n\\[\n\\sin \\left( 2\\pi - \\frac{2\\pi}{3} \\right) = -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\nPutting it all together, we have:\n\\[\n\\sin \\left( \\frac{11\\pi}{3} \\right) = -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\nSo, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}\n\\]",
