      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "(a) Apply the reference angle $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$ which is between $0$ and $\\pi/2$. Observe that it has an inscribed 60-degree angle in the unit circle, spanning across an arc of length $\\frac{1}{3}$ of the circle.\n(b) Since $\\frac{11 \\pi}{3}$ is an angle greater than $2\\pi$ radians (because $\\pi > 30'$), subtract $2\\pi = \\frac{2\\pi \\cdot 3}{3}$ from it. The reason is that adding or subtracting multiples of $2\\pi$ radians doesn't change the angle.\n(c) The arc that this expression spans is $\\frac{11\\pi}{3} - 2\\pi = \\frac{\\pi}{3}$.\n(d) The value of $\\sin$ of a vertical line on our unit circle is 0 since we are on the y axis.\n(e) Using (b) and (d) together, it follows that function-$\\color{white}{\\text{^}} \\sin \\left( \\frac{11 \\pi}{3} \\right) = \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{3} = \\boxed{\\frac{\\sqrt 3}{2}}$.",
